# radius — example environment file.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Everything here is OPTIONAL. The app runs with no .env at all; these only
# change defaults. Vite reads this file at build time (and on `npm run dev`),
# so a change needs a restart to take effect.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️  NOTHING SECRET GOES IN THIS FILE.
#
# Vite inlines every VITE_-prefixed variable into the JavaScript bundle it
# ships to the browser. A value put here is readable by anyone who opens
# devtools or fetches dist/assets/*.js — it is published, not configured.
# That is why the only variable below is a hostname.
#
# In particular, the TrueFoundry PAT does NOT belong here. See "The other .env"
# at the bottom for where it actually lives.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# --- TrueFoundry ------------------------------------------------------------

# Prefills the gateway URL in the Providers dialog and the Usage dialog's
# "Gateway usage" section, for a browser that hasn't saved one yet.
#
# Leave it unset and both fields start empty and ask, which is the right
# behaviour for anyone who isn't on a specific deployment. Set it if your team
# always points at the same gateway and typing it every time is friction.
#
# This is a default, not a lock: whatever the user saves in the dialog is kept
# in localStorage and always wins over this value.
#
# Read by src/lib/truefoundry.js (DEFAULT_TRUEFOUNDRY_GATEWAY).
VITE_TRUEFOUNDRY_GATEWAY=https://gateway.example.com


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not read from this file
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The variables below are real and useful, but nothing loads them out of a
# .env — set them in your shell or on the command line. They are listed here
# only so that putting them in .env and wondering why nothing happened isn't a
# thing that has to be discovered.
#
#   MOCK_PORT                 Port for test/mock-opencode.js. Defaults to 4096,
#                             which is what playwright.config.js and the Vite
#                             proxy expect — change both or neither.
#
#   PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_PATH  Use an existing Chromium instead of the one
#                             `npx playwright install` downloads. Needed on
#                             sandboxes and CI images that ship a build whose
#                             number doesn't match this package's:
#                               PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_PATH=/path/to/chromium npm test
#
#   CI                        Set by CI. Makes Playwright start its own servers
#                             rather than reusing ones you already have running.
#
# The OpenCode server's own connection settings are NOT environment variables
# either — port, username and password are entered in the connect dialog and
# persisted in localStorage, because they are per-browser and change when you
# re-point an SSH forward. See src/stores/ssh.js.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The other .env  (a different file, on a different machine)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Confusingly, this app also READS a .env — but not this one. To save you
# re-typing a TrueFoundry PAT, the Providers dialog looks for one on the
# OpenCode *host*, over PTY, in:
#
#   <the session's project directory>/.env
#   ~/.config/opencode/.env
#
# under any of these keys:
#
#   TRUEFOUNDRY_API_KEY=tfy-...
#   TRUEFOUNDRY_PAT=tfy-...
#   TFY_API_KEY=tfy-...
#
# That file belongs to the machine running `opencode2 serve`, and the token
# never reaches this repo, this bundle, or browser storage. Putting the PAT in
# the .env next to this example would publish it in the JS bundle and still not
# be found. See loadEnvPAT() in src/stores/providers.js and docs/truefoundry.md.
